{"module":"Term 1","title":"Unit 3 — Food","description":"Food vocabulary, countable and uncountable nouns, quantifiers, ordering in a restaurant.","intro":"This unit is about food, eating habits and healthy diets.","objectifs":["Name food and meals.","Use countable / uncountable nouns with quantifiers.","Order food and make polite requests."],"cours":[{"t":"Vocabulary","h":"<ul><li>meals: breakfast, lunch, dinner, a snack</li><li>containers: a bottle of, a loaf of, a kilo of, a packet of, a slice of</li><li>a balanced diet, junk food, vegetarian, calories, vitamins</li></ul>"},{"t":"Grammar: countable / uncountable and quantifiers","h":"<table><tr><th></th><th>Countable (apples)</th><th>Uncountable (water, bread)</th></tr><tr><td>Positive</td><td>some / a lot of / many</td><td>some / a lot of / much (rare)</td></tr><tr><td>Negative / question</td><td>any / many</td><td>any / much</td></tr><tr><td>Small quantity</td><td>a few</td><td>a little</td></tr></table><div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"box-title\">Examples</span><p>How <b>many</b> eggs? — How <b>much</b> sugar? There are <b>a few</b> oranges and <b>a little</b> milk.</p></div>"},{"t":"Communication: ordering food","h":"<ul><li>Waiter: “Are you ready to order?” — “I’d like the fish, please.”</li><li>“Could I have some water, please?” / “Can I have the bill, please?”</li></ul>"}],"recap":"Countable → many, a few, how many. Uncountable → much, a little, how much. Some in positive sentences (and offers), any in negatives and questions. Polite requests: I’d like…, Could I have…?","cards":[["How many?","Countable nouns: how many apples?"],["How much?","Uncountable nouns: how much rice?"],["a few / a little","few + countable ; little + uncountable"],["I’d like…","Polite way to order"]],"exercises":{"facile":[{"type":"qcm","q":"How ___ water do you drink a day?","choices":["much","many","few","a few"],"answer":0,"explain":"“Water” is uncountable → much."},{"type":"direct","q":"Complete: a ___ of bread (container word).","accept":["loaf"],"display":"loaf","explain":"We say “a loaf of bread”."},{"type":"qcm","q":"Pizza, burgers and crisps are examples of…","choices":["junk food","a balanced diet","vitamins","vegetarian food"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fast, unhealthy food = junk food."}],"moyen":[{"type":"qcm","q":"There aren’t ___ eggs in the fridge.","choices":["any","some","much","a little"],"answer":0,"explain":"Negative sentence with a countable noun → any."},{"type":"qcm","q":"Which is the most polite?","choices":["Could I have a glass of juice, please?","Give me juice.","I want juice now.","Juice!"],"answer":0,"explain":"“Could I have…, please?” is a polite request."},{"type":"direct","q":"Complete: There is only a ___ sugar left. (little / few)","accept":["little"],"display":"little","explain":"“Sugar” is uncountable → a little."}],"difficile":[{"type":"qcm","q":"Find the correct sentence:","choices":["I need some information about the menu.","I need some informations about the menu.","I need an information about the menu.","I need many informations about the menu."],"answer":0,"explain":"“Information” is uncountable: no plural, no “an”."},{"type":"direct","q":"Complete: “Would you like ___ tea?” (offer)","accept":["some"],"display":"some","explain":"In offers and requests we use “some” even in questions."},{"type":"qcm","q":"“He eats too ___ sweets.”","choices":["many","much","little","any"],"answer":0,"explain":"“Sweets” is countable plural → too many."}]}}